Title: The Velveteen Rabbit
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from the book by Margery Williams
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - DAY

A cozy, sunlit Victorian nursery. Stuffed animals and tin toys line the shelves.

Sitting on the rug is the VELVETEEN RABBIT—a fat, soft toy rabbit with brown and white spotted fur, thread whiskers, and pink sateen ears.

Beside him stands the SKIN HORSE, an ancient leather toy horse with bald patches on his coat and seams showing beneath his worn padding.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was once a velveteen rabbit, and in the beginning he was really splendid. He was fat and bunchy, as a rabbit should be; his coat was spotted brown and white, he had real thread whiskers, and his ears were lined with pink sateen.

A MECHANICAL BOAT, bright tin with peeling paint, clicks along the wooden floor past them.

MECHANICAL BOAT
Mind my rigging! I am constructed with authentic nautical gear, unlike sawdust-stuffed novelties.

The Velveteen Rabbit tucked his paws in, looking down at his soft seams.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

The Skin Horse gently turns his head toward the Rabbit.

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you. When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time. That's why it doesn't happen often to people who break easily, or have sharp edges, or who have to be carefully kept.

The Skin Horse smiles kindly at the small Rabbit.

SKIN HORSE
Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out and you get loose in the joints and very shabby. But these things don't matter at all, because once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
The Boy's Uncle made you Real, didn't he?

SKIN HORSE
That was a great many years ago. But once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

A small night-light glows softly on the mantelpiece.

NANA, a stern nanny in a dark dress, swoops through the open doorway, sweeping toys off the floor.

NANA
Where has that china dog gotten to? Always misplaced at bedtime!

She grabs the Velveteen Rabbit from a chair by one ear and thrusts him into the arms of the BOY, who lies tucked under the blankets.

NANA
Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

Nana marches out and closes the door.

The Boy pulls the Velveteen Rabbit close under his chin. He lifts the blanket to make a cavern.

BOY
Look, Bunny, a burrow. Just like where the wild rabbits live.

The Rabbit nestles snugly against the Boy's cheek.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That night, and for many nights after, the Velveteen Rabbit slept in the Boy's bed. And so time went on, and the little Rabbit was very happy—so happy that he never noticed how his beautiful velveteen fur was getting shabbier and shabbier, and all the pink rubbed off his nose where the Boy had kissed him.

EXT. GARDEN - DAY

Sunlight filters through raspberry canes behind a flower border.

The Boy carries the Rabbit in a wooden wheelbarrow, then sets him down in a cozy nook of green leaves.

NANA (O.S.)
Master William! Tea time! Come inside at once!

The Boy jumps up and runs off toward the house.

BOY
Coming, Nana!

The Rabbit sits tucked in the green shade as afternoon turns to twilight. Dew collects on his velvet coat.

Hours pass. Later, dark falls. The light of a candle cuts through the dusk.

Nana walks into the garden holding a brass candlestick, leading the tearful Boy by the hand.

NANA
Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

She spots the Rabbit damp in the grass, picks him up, and wipes the mud off him with her apron.

BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

The Rabbit's boot-button eyes gleam softly in the candlelight.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When the little Rabbit heard that he was happy, for he knew that what the Skin Horse had said was true at last. The nursery magic had happened to him, and he was a toy no longer.

EXT. WOODS - EVENING

Golden summer evening light filters through tall ferns and bracken in a quiet forest glade.

The Velveteen Rabbit sits nestled in the bracken where the Boy placed him. Nearby, small ants walk across his velvet paws.

Rustling sounds break the quiet. Two WILD RABBITS creep out from the tall fern fronds. Their brown fur is sleek, their nostrils twitch, and their hind legs flex with effortless muscle.

The wild rabbits approach the Velveteen Rabbit, sniffing the air.

WILD RABBIT
Why don't you get up and play with us?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

The Wild Rabbit rears up on his hind legs and leaps in a wide circle.

WILD RABBIT
It's as easy as anything! Can you hop on your hind legs?

The Velveteen Rabbit remains seated in the fern stem, tucking his seamless cloth body down tight.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't want to.

The Wild Rabbit leans in close, inspecting the Velveteen Rabbit's tail end.

WILD RABBIT
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I have! I have got hind legs! I am sitting on them!

WILD RABBIT
Then stretch them out and show me, like this!

The Wild Rabbit whirls around in a dizzying leap. He leans in closer, brushing his whiskers against the Velveteen Rabbit's ear, then instantly leaps backward with a wrinkled nose.

WILD RABBIT
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I am Real! I am Real! The Boy said so!

Heavy footsteps crash through the dry leaves.

The Boy bursts into the clearing, chasing a butterfly.

With a flash of white tails, the two wild rabbits bound away into the thicket.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

Only the rustling ferns answer. The Velveteen Rabbit sits motionless in the grass.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

A dim lamp burns on the bedside table. Medicines and glass bottles cluster on the tray.

The Boy lies in bed, flushed with fever, tossing and turning.

Underneath the edge of the blanket, the Velveteen Rabbit is nestled against the Boy's ribs, worn grey and threadbare.

DOCTOR (O.S.)
The fever has broken at last. He will recover nicely now.

NANA (O.S.)
Thank heaven. We must prepare him for the seaside trip tomorrow as you ordered.

The DOCTOR walks into view beside Nana at the foot of the bed.

NANA
What about his old Bunny?

The Doctor uses his cane to lift the corner of the sheet, eyeing the frayed, threadbare toy.

DOCTOR
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs! Burn it at once. Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more!

The Velveteen Rabbit lies frozen beneath the doctor's cane.

EXT. BEHIND FOWL-HOUSE - NIGHT

Moonlight shines down on a pile of discarded rubbish, old picture books, and an open burlap sack behind a wooden chicken coop.

The Velveteen Rabbit wriggles his head out through the mouth of the sack. His fur is thin and threadbare, his whiskers gone, his pink ear lining turned grey.

In the distance, the dark silhouette of the garden's raspberry canes sways in the night wind.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Of what use was it to be loved and lose one's beauty and become Real if it all ended like this?

A single clear tear wells in the Rabbit's boot-button eye, trickles down his worn velvet nose, and drops onto the soil.

Where the tear hits the earth, a green shoot instantly pierces the ground. It unwinds emerald leaves and opens into a luminous golden cup-shaped blossom.

Light pours from the petals. Out of the heart of the flower steps the NURSERY FAIRY, draped in shimmering dew-drop robes with flowers in her hair.

She kneels and scoops the Velveteen Rabbit into her arms, kissing his damp velvet nose.

NURSERY FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I think so... but I can't remember where I have seen you.

NURSERY FAIRY
I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved. When they are old and worn out and the children don't need them any more, then I come and take them away with me and turn them into Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

NURSERY FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to every one.

EXT. FOREST GLADE - NIGHT

Bright moonlight floods the open clearing.

The Nursery Fairy cradles the Velveteen Rabbit as she descends onto the soft grass.

A circle of wild rabbits stops dancing and gathers around in awe.

NURSERY FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land, for he is going to live with you for ever and ever!

She sets the Velveteen Rabbit gently down upon the turf.

NURSERY FAIRY
Run and play, little Rabbit!

The Velveteen Rabbit sits motionless, looking down shyly at his front paws.

A small woodland fly buzzes near his face and tickles his nose.

Without thinking, the Rabbit lifts his hind foot to scratch it.

He freezes. He looks down.

Where his stiff cloth back used to be, two strong, furry hind legs extend onto the grass. His coat is soft, warm brown fur. His ears twitch freely in the breeze, and long whiskers brush the grass.

He pushes off the ground—and leaps high into the air.

He lands softly, whirls around, and springs across the turf in wild delight among the dancing wild rabbits.

He turns back to look, but the Fairy has vanished into the glowing moonlight.

EXT. WOODS - DAY

Spring sunlight streams through fresh green leaves. Wild flowers bloom along the forest floor.

The Boy, older now and healthy, walks along the forest path holding a wooden walking stick.

He stops near a patch of tall ferns.

Two wild rabbits creep out from the bracken and gaze up at him.

One rabbit is brown all over. The other has subtle, beautiful markings under his fur, faint spots of brown and white visible beneath his soft coat. His black eyes shine with ancient wisdom.

The spotted rabbit twitches his nose, steps closer to the Boy, and looks up into his face.

The Boy lowers his stick, peering closely at the wild rabbit.

BOY
Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!

The spotted rabbit gives a joyous hop, turns, and bounds away into the sunlit woods with his companion.

The Boy watches him go, smiling into the warm spring light.

FADE OUT.
